## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`options`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is configured. Live Preview requires a `live_preview` config block (preview_token, management_token, enable) passed into `contentstack.Stack(options)`. The current `options` object (lines 55-67) lacks any `live_preview` configuration, so the Stack is initialized in delivery-only mode and can never serve draft/preview content — the root cause of why CMS live preview does not work.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add `live_preview` configuration to the `options` object before it is passed to `contentstack.Stack()`. Read `CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN` and `CONTENTSTACK_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN` from `process.env` (add them alongside the existing env destructuring at lines 17-24). Then extend `options` with: `live_preview: { preview_token: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN || '', management_token: CONTENTSTACK_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN || '', enable: !!CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN }`. This is the minimal change — the Contentstack SDK's `Stack` constructor accepts `live_preview` natively; no new functions or architecture are needed. The `isTestEnv` guard pattern already used in this file (line 28) can be reused for any env-validation of the new tokens if desired.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a tester opens a page in CMS live-preview mode after saving a draft (unpublished) change to a content entry, the rendered page displays the draft content rather than the last published version.
- While a live-preview session is open, a tester who edits and saves a field in the CMS sees the updated value appear on the rendered page without redeploying or restarting the application.
- When the same page is visited outside of any preview session, it renders only the published CMS content, with no draft or unpublished values visible.
- When a page is opened in preview mode with an invalid preview token or for content that has no draft version, the page still renders successfully showing the published content, with no error screen, blank page, or crash.
- Draft content shown in preview mode appears in the same location, formatting, and styling on the page as the published content it replaces, so the preview accurately represents how the change will look once published.
- All pages and sections on the site that display CMS-managed content render draft values when viewed in live-preview mode, with no visible layout breakage or missing content anywhere draft changes exist.

